.model D D
.lib C:\Users\bubba\AppData\Local\LTspice\lib\cmp\standard.dio
.model NPN NPN
.model PNP PNP
.lib C:\Users\bubba\AppData\Local\LTspice\lib\cmp\standard.bjt
.lib TLV9001.lib
.ic V(Out)=0 V(Out-)=1 V(C10)=0 V(C5)=0 V(C2)=0
.include MCP6561.lib
.tran 30m startup uic
.ic I(L1)=0 V(0A_hi)=5
.lib CD4000_v.lib
.lib HYG180N10.lib
.options gmin=100f reltol=0.001
.lib INA241A4.lib
.model LED_RED   D(IS=1.18e-20 N=18 RS=3 CJO=2n)
.model LED_GREEN D(IS=9.5e-25  N=22 RS=4 CJO=2n)
.model LED_BLUE  D(IS=9.5e-25  N=22 RS=4 CJO=2n)
.model 100W_LED_BLUE D(IS=1e-24 N=20.5 RS=0.8 CJO=2n)
.model 100W_LED_RED D(IS=1e-23 N=14.5 RS=0.6 CJO=2n)
.model 100W_LED_GREEN D(IS=1.5e-24 N=20.5 RS=0.9 CJO=2n)
.lib L78L05.lib
.lib MC7812.lib
.meas TRAN Ptvs AVG  (-1)*V(LX)*I(D19) FROM 2m TO 13m
.meas TRAN Etvs INTEG (-1)*V(LX)*I(D19)
* .ic V(Vout_1)=30 V(Vout_2)=38 V(Vout_3)=30
.ic V(SRV1)=2.89 V(SRV2)=3.86 V(SRV3)=3.86
.ic V(Vref_1)=2 V(Vref_2)=3 V(Vref_3)=3
* Start-up state: the two .ic lines above start the servos and reference filters settled.\nDelete both for a true cold start (about 0.5 s to full brightness).
.lib CD74HC4066.lib
.lib 74HC4051.lib
.lib MCP4451.lib
.lib CSS4J.lib
.lib TVS_5p0SMDJ.lib
.param IREF1_DUTY=256 IREF2_DUTY=256 IREF3_DUTY=256
.param POT1_CODE=86 POT2_CODE=103 POT3_CODE=103
* IREFn_DUTY: Arduino PWM duty on IREFn (J9) in 256ths, 490 Hz: 256 = always on, 128 = 50 %, 0 = off.\nPOTn_CODE: MCP4451 wiper code of U26A/B/C, 0-256 (0 = wiper at B = full current, 256 = at A = 0 A;
* I added a lot of capacitors for individual components in the kicad, which are all put here
* confirmed in Microchip's data sheet DS22267A). 86/103/103 give 2.63/2.37/2.37 A peaks.\nParts named SIM_ exist only in the simulation (battery, cable, LEDs, Arduino outputs).\nDimming runs (IREFn_DUTY below 256) can stop advancing while all LEDs are off (log fills with\n"tolerance relaxed" at one time). Adding solver=alt to the .options line got past it (2-3x slower).
.model S2MW D(IS=14n RS=17m N=1.8 CJO=25p M=0.333 TT=3u BV=1000 IBV=5u)
* simulation only - not on the PCB
* D28-D30: pre-charge diodes Vin -> Vout_1/2/3
* actual led diode\nwill not be on the pcb
* simulation only - not on the PCB\n0-5V PWM signal sent from arduino to control brightness
* U26E: MCP4451 control unit, serves U26A/B/C
* Channel 1 output-voltage error amplifier, Vout_1 -> Verr1
* 0-5V PWM to set minimum voltage reference\nThis will be connected to an arduino pin
* this pushes up the reference voltage if the \noutput voltage is undershooting for a long time
* digital potentiometer \nto control current
* switch used so that circuit \nonly samples while the led is on
* error signal - used to control how long the inductor charges up for
* mosfet used as a kind of variable resistor \nto keep led current from going too high.
* op amp will try to \nmake its output match \nthe reference current
* PWM duty = IREF1_DUTY/256 (.param)
* 1 mOhm shunt:\nmeasures inductor current
* current sense\namplifies R1 voltage by 100x
* low side switch
* clamps LX spikes
* connects LX to output 1
* connects LX to output 2
* connects LX to output 3
* filters the PWM to DC
* U26A: one of the 4 pots inside U26 (MCP4451)
* OUTPUT 1
* OUTPUT 2
* OUTPUT 3
* CH1 LED CURRENT LIMIT
* CH1 HEADROOM SERVO
* when m1 pulls inductor to ground, \ncurrent can conduct through the mosfet \nbody diode to charge up this capacitor
* actual led diode\nwill not be on the pcb
* simulation only - not on the PCB
* Channel 2 output-voltage error amplifier, Vout_2 -> Verr2
* simulation only - not on the PCB
* PWM duty = IREF2_DUTY/256 (.param)
* U26B: one of the 4 pots inside U26 (MCP4451)
* CH2 LED CURRENT LIMIT
* CH2 HEADROOM SERVO
* same as channel 1
* actual led diode\nwill not be on the pcb
* simulation only - not on the PCB
* Channel 3 output-voltage error amplifier, Vout_3 -> Verr3
* simulation only - not on the PCB
* PWM duty = IREF3_DUTY/256 (.param)
* U26C: one of the 4 pots inside U26 (MCP4451)
* CH3 LED CURRENT LIMIT
* CH3 HEADROOM SERVO
* same as channel 1
* LOW-ERROR TIMEOUT
* SEQUENCER / ERROR SELECT
* M1 ON/OFF LATCH
* CHANNEL ENABLE
* M1 INHIBIT
* simulation only - not on the PCB
* makes it so that if there is one channel with low error, \nit doesn't just stop on that one channel.
* rc circuit delays m1 turn on\ndiode creates fast turn off
* inhibits m1 if 12V rail is below 9.8V
* disables m1 if there is 0 \nerror on the current channel
* Signal that can be sent from the \narduino to disable the circuit
